\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Mean AUROC of every detector (rows, ordered by overall combined mean) on the three TSB-StreamingAD categories DRIFT, OOD, and STABLE (columns). Brighter cells denote higher AUROC. The top rows --- feature distance/density and feature-manifold detectors --- are bright across all three categories, while the bottom rows --- post-hoc softmax, logit, activation, and gradient detectors, together with the seasonal SRS --- are uniformly dark, indicating scores at or below the \num {0.5} chance line. Among the leaders, the STABLE specificity controls and the subtle DRIFT regime are separated best (Mahalanobis \num {0.855} STABLE, \num {0.858} DRIFT; DFM-PCA \num {0.849} STABLE), while the post-hoc cluster is darkest on STABLE, where it over-flags the very control windows a calibrated detector should leave alone.}}{31}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:heatmap}{{1}{31}{Mean AUROC of every detector (rows, ordered by overall combined mean) on the three TSB-StreamingAD categories DRIFT, OOD, and STABLE (columns). Brighter cells denote higher AUROC. The top rows --- feature distance/density and feature-manifold detectors --- are bright across all three categories, while the bottom rows --- post-hoc softmax, logit, activation, and gradient detectors, together with the seasonal SRS --- are uniformly dark, indicating scores at or below the \num {0.5} chance line. Among the leaders, the STABLE specificity controls and the subtle DRIFT regime are separated best (Mahalanobis \num {0.855} STABLE, \num {0.858} DRIFT; DFM-PCA \num {0.849} STABLE), while the post-hoc cluster is darkest on STABLE, where it over-flags the very control windows a calibrated detector should leave alone}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:heatmap@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][12][]31}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Mean AUROC per detector on TSB-StreamingAD-U versus TSB-StreamingAD-M. The feature distance/density and feature-manifold detectors (upper band) cluster tightly above \num {0.59} on both families, whereas the post-hoc softmax, logit, activation, and gradient detectors (lower band) fall below the \num {0.5} chance line on both, several with inverted scores. The two clusters are cleanly separated with no overlap; the post-hoc cluster is marginally less inverted on the multivariate family.}}{32}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:splitbar}{{2}{32}{Mean AUROC per detector on TSB-StreamingAD-U versus TSB-StreamingAD-M. The feature distance/density and feature-manifold detectors (upper band) cluster tightly above \num {0.59} on both families, whereas the post-hoc softmax, logit, activation, and gradient detectors (lower band) fall below the \num {0.5} chance line on both, several with inverted scores. The two clusters are cleanly separated with no overlap; the post-hoc cluster is marginally less inverted on the multivariate family}{figure.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:splitbar@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][13][]32}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Mean AUROC under global (Source-1) normalisation versus per-series normalisation, per detector. Feature distance/density detectors (upper points) gain sharply under global normalisation; post-hoc softmax/logit/gradient detectors (lower points) remain inverted under both schemes and are slightly less inverted under per-series normalisation. The two clusters move in opposite directions --- a dichotomy, not a shared preprocessing preference.}}{33}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:norm}{{3}{33}{Mean AUROC under global (Source-1) normalisation versus per-series normalisation, per detector. Feature distance/density detectors (upper points) gain sharply under global normalisation; post-hoc softmax/logit/gradient detectors (lower points) remain inverted under both schemes and are slightly less inverted under per-series normalisation. The two clusters move in opposite directions --- a dichotomy, not a shared preprocessing preference}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:norm@cref}{{[figure][3][]3}{[1][15][]33}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Accuracy versus inference cost (time axis on log scale). Each point is a detector; the upper-left region is best. Mahalanobis, DFM-PCA, DEEDEE, and CatSight hold the Pareto front, attaining the highest mean AUROC at roughly \SIrange {1}{4}{\milli \second } per window, whereas CODiT ($\sim $\SI {28}{\milli \second }), DiffAD ($\sim $\SI {22}{\milli \second }), and SRS ($\sim $\SI {16}{\milli \second }) are far slower without a corresponding accuracy gain.}}{34}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:efficiency}{{4}{34}{Accuracy versus inference cost (time axis on log scale). Each point is a detector; the upper-left region is best. Mahalanobis, DFM-PCA, DEEDEE, and CatSight hold the Pareto front, attaining the highest mean AUROC at roughly \SIrange {1}{4}{\milli \second } per window, whereas CODiT ($\sim $\SI {28}{\milli \second }), DiffAD ($\sim $\SI {22}{\milli \second }), and SRS ($\sim $\SI {16}{\milli \second }) are far slower without a corresponding accuracy gain}{figure.4}{}}
\newlabel{fig:efficiency@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][15][]34}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Critical-difference diagram (Nemenyi post-hoc, $\alpha = 0.05$) over the \num {16} detectors excluding SRS on the complete-case pool of $N = \num {515}$ datasets. Detectors are positioned by mean rank (rank\nobreakspace  {}\num {1}, best, at the top); a crossbar joins detectors whose mean ranks differ by less than the critical difference (CD${} = \num {1.016}$). The feature-manifold detectors (Mahalanobis \num {4.35}, DFM-PCA \num {4.79}, then the InvAD--M2N2 band) are cleanly separated from the post-hoc cluster (mean ranks \numrange {10.38}{11.33}), which is itself a statistical tie internally.}}{34}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cd_diagram}{{5}{34}{Critical-difference diagram (Nemenyi post-hoc, $\alpha = 0.05$) over the \num {16} detectors excluding SRS on the complete-case pool of $N = \num {515}$ datasets. Detectors are positioned by mean rank (rank~\num {1}, best, at the top); a crossbar joins detectors whose mean ranks differ by less than the critical difference (CD${} = \num {1.016}$). The feature-manifold detectors (Mahalanobis \num {4.35}, DFM-PCA \num {4.79}, then the InvAD--M2N2 band) are cleanly separated from the post-hoc cluster (mean ranks \numrange {10.38}{11.33}), which is itself a statistical tie internally}{figure.5}{}}
\newlabel{fig:cd_diagram@cref}{{[figure][5][]5}{[1][18][]34}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Nemenyi pairwise-significance sign-plot over the \num {16} detectors (ordered by mean rank). A coloured off-diagonal cell marks a significant pairwise difference at $\alpha = 0.05$; the dark block separating the feature-manifold detectors (upper-left) from the post-hoc cluster (lower-right) shows that every cross-family contrast is significant, while the post-hoc detectors are mutually indistinguishable.}}{35}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:signplot}{{6}{35}{Nemenyi pairwise-significance sign-plot over the \num {16} detectors (ordered by mean rank). A coloured off-diagonal cell marks a significant pairwise difference at $\alpha = 0.05$; the dark block separating the feature-manifold detectors (upper-left) from the post-hoc cluster (lower-right) shows that every cross-family contrast is significant, while the post-hoc detectors are mutually indistinguishable}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:signplot@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][18][]35}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Mean AUROC by data-source domain (rows, with dataset counts) and detector (columns, grouped feature-manifold, then ts-specific, then post-hoc). Green marks strong separation, red marks inversion. The left (feature-manifold) block is green across almost every domain; the right (post-hoc) block is red across almost every domain. The PSM row inverts the pattern --- red on the left, green on the right --- the single domain where the post-hoc family outperforms the feature family.}}{36}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:per_domain_heatmap}{{7}{36}{Mean AUROC by data-source domain (rows, with dataset counts) and detector (columns, grouped feature-manifold, then ts-specific, then post-hoc). Green marks strong separation, red marks inversion. The left (feature-manifold) block is green across almost every domain; the right (post-hoc) block is red across almost every domain. The PSM row inverts the pattern --- red on the left, green on the right --- the single domain where the post-hoc family outperforms the feature family}{figure.7}{}}
\newlabel{fig:per_domain_heatmap@cref}{{[figure][7][]7}{[1][19][]36}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Per-method fidelity verdict and evaluation protocol for the twenty-four audited candidate detectors. All twenty-four are legitimate OOD methods from the literature audited for fidelity; the final column indicates the \emph  {evaluation protocol}, not exclusion. The \emph  {fidelity verdict} records the audit outcome: \textsc  {Faithful} --- the implementation matches the source paper (and official code where available) as-is; \textsc  {Corrected} --- a discrepancy that materially changed the OOD score was repaired with a validated, paper-faithful fix (an \texttt  {\_enh} variant, \cref  {sec:methods}); \textsc  {Adaptation} --- the faithful mechanism was recovered but relabelled or restricted to remain well-defined on the frozen-backbone protocol. The \emph  {evaluation} column states the protocol under which the detector is evaluated: \emph  {Unified} for the seventeen compatible with the shared fair-comparison streaming protocol, or \emph  {Adapted} for the seven whose defining mechanism requires a method-appropriate protocol (auxiliary-outlier training, an ordered stream, a retrained feature extractor, or batch-level scoring) and are reported separately (\cref  {tab:classd}, \cref  {tab:class_d_appendix}). All five post-hoc/activation/gradient methods that a prior draft marked ``not verified'' (ReAct, DICE, SCALE, GradNorm) and DiffAD were either corrected or confirmed faithful and are evaluated under the unified protocol; the three augmented-training methods (Outlier Exposure, DivOE, DiverseMix) require an auxiliary-outlier training regime the unified protocol cannot provide, so they are evaluated under adapted arms.}}{37}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:audit}{{8}{37}{Per-method fidelity verdict and evaluation protocol for the twenty-four audited candidate detectors. All twenty-four are legitimate OOD methods from the literature audited for fidelity; the final column indicates the \emph {evaluation protocol}, not exclusion. The \emph {fidelity verdict} records the audit outcome: \textsc {Faithful} --- the implementation matches the source paper (and official code where available) as-is; \textsc {Corrected} --- a discrepancy that materially changed the OOD score was repaired with a validated, paper-faithful fix (an \texttt {\_enh} variant, \cref {sec:methods}); \textsc {Adaptation} --- the faithful mechanism was recovered but relabelled or restricted to remain well-defined on the frozen-backbone protocol. The \emph {evaluation} column states the protocol under which the detector is evaluated: \emph {Unified} for the seventeen compatible with the shared fair-comparison streaming protocol, or \emph {Adapted} for the seven whose defining mechanism requires a method-appropriate protocol (auxiliary-outlier training, an ordered stream, a retrained feature extractor, or batch-level scoring) and are reported separately (\cref {tab:classd}, \cref {tab:class_d_appendix}). All five post-hoc/activation/gradient methods that a prior draft marked ``not verified'' (ReAct, DICE, SCALE, GradNorm) and DiffAD were either corrected or confirmed faithful and are evaluated under the unified protocol; the three augmented-training methods (Outlier Exposure, DivOE, DiverseMix) require an auxiliary-outlier training regime the unified protocol cannot provide, so they are evaluated under adapted arms}{table.8}{}}
\newlabel{tab:audit@cref}{{[table][8][2147483647]8}{[1][29][]37}}
